// `go c.m()` — a method call in a `go` statement, for BOTH receiver kinds.
//
// This did not compile. codegen_generate_go_stmt evaluates the callee with
// codegen_generate_expression on the selector, which reaches
// codegen_generate_method_value. That function took the receiver-stripped
// signature from expr->node_type, and the type checker only strips in VALUE
// position (expression_checker.c). In `go c.m()` the selector is a call
// callee, so node_type kept the receiver as params[0], and the bound thunk
// then handed the method one argument too many:
//
//   Module verification failed: Incorrect number of arguments passed to
//   called function!  call void @C__run(ptr %bound_recv, ptr %1)
//
// The ledger recorded this as "on a pointer receiver". It was not: a VALUE
// receiver produced the identical error, while a plain method value
// (`f := c.m` then `f()`) with a pointer receiver compiled and ran correctly.
// The defect was the `go` statement path, not the receiver kind — which is
// why this probe covers a pointer receiver, a value receiver, AND the
// method-value path that already worked and must keep working.
//
// The channels are CAPTURED IN THE RECEIVER rather than passed as arguments,
// because `go f(args)` on a function value is a separate, deliberate v1
// restriction ("go with argument-carrying function values is not yet
// supported; capture the values instead"). Passing them would test that
// restriction instead of this fix.
//
// Output is synchronised on purpose. A goroutine racing main's print would
// make this fixture flaky, and a flaky golden is worse than no golden.
// Expected output comes from `go run` on the equivalent Go program, never
// hand-written.
package main

import "fmt"

type Counter struct {
	n    int
	done chan int
}

// Pointer receiver: mutates through the receiver, so the goroutine must act
// on the same object main allocated, not a copy.
func (c *Counter) bumpAndSend() {
	c.n++
	c.done <- c.n
}

type Tag struct {
	label string
	out   chan string
}

// Value receiver: the goroutine gets its own copy.
func (t Tag) send() {
	t.out <- t.label
}

func main() {
	c := &Counter{n: 41, done: make(chan int)}
	go c.bumpAndSend()
	fmt.Println(<-c.done)

	t := Tag{label: "value-recv", out: make(chan string)}
	go t.send()
	fmt.Println(<-t.out)

	// The method-value path. This already worked before the fix, and the 43
	// proves the pointer receiver above really did mutate the shared object.
	f := c.bumpAndSend
	go f()
	fmt.Println(<-c.done)
}
